sources:
    {"organization":"Britannica","title":"Edward Teller","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Edward-Teller"}

definition:
    Hungarian-American theoretical physicist (1908–2003), known as the 'father of the hydrogen bomb'. After fleeing Nazi Germany and working on the Manhattan Project's fission weapons, he drove the 1951 Ulam–Teller staged-compression idea that made thermonuclear weapons feasible — the Ivy Mike shot (1952) proved it. He co-founded Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory and championed nuclear weapons testing, rocket propulsion, and civil defense. His 1954 testimony that Robert Oppenheimer was a security risk split the scientific community and remains controversial.

debunking_snippet:
    “The hydrogen bomb was one man's invention.” It took many hands — Stanisław Ulam's compression scheme was as essential as Teller's vision; both names are on the key 1951 paper.
